    ("For the reaction $2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)$, $\\Delta H = -483.6\\text{ kJ}$. What is the standard enthalpy of formation of water vapor ($\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)]$)?",
     "$-241.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-483.6\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+241.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-967.2\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "The given equation produces $2\\text{ moles}$ of $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)$. By definition, standard enthalpy of formation is for ONE mole: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ = \\frac{-483.6}{2} = -241.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Molar Formation Enthalpy of Steam"),
    ("Given: $\\Delta_{\\text{fus}} H^\\circ[\\text{H}_2\\text{O}] = +6.01\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_{\\text{vap}} H^\\circ[\\text{H}_2\\text{O}] = +40.66\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ at $0^\\circ\\text{C}$ and $100^\\circ\\text{C}$ respectively. Assuming values remain constant, the enthalpy of sublimation of ice is approximately:",
     "$+46.67\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+34.65\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-46.67\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+244.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "$\\Delta_{\\text{sub}} H^\\circ = \\Delta_{\\text{fus}} H^\\circ + \\Delta_{\\text{vap}} H^\\circ = 6.01 + 40.66 = +46.67\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Enthalpy of Sublimation of Ice"),
    ("Consider the thermochemical equations:\n(1) $\\text{C}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -394\\text{ kJ}$\n(2) $2\\text{CO}(g) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{CO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -566\\text{ kJ}$\nWhat is the enthalpy change for: $\\text{C}(s) + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CO}(g)$?",
     "$-111\\text{ kJ}$", "$+111\\text{ kJ}$", "$-172\\text{ kJ}$", "$-677\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Equation (1) minus $\\frac{1}{2}$ Equation (2): $\\Delta H = -394 - \\frac{1}{2}(-566) = -394 + 283 = -111\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "CO Formation from Hess's Law"),
    ("The calorific values of petrol, coal, and methane are approximately $48\\text{ kJ g}^{-1}$, $30\\text{ kJ g}^{-1}$, and $55\\text{ kJ g}^{-1}$ respectively. Which fuel releases the maximum heat per gram?",
     "Methane", "Petrol", "Coal", "All release the same amount", "A",
     "Methane has a calorific value of $55\\text{ kJ g}^{-1}$, which is higher than petrol ($48\\text{ kJ g}^{-1}$) and coal ($30\\text{ kJ g}^{-1}$).",
     "Calorific Value Comparison"),
    ("If $5.0\\text{ g}$ of liquid water at $100^\\circ\\text{C}$ is converted into steam at $100^\\circ\\text{C}$, the heat required is: (Given: $\\Delta_{\\text{vap}} H = 40.66\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$, molar mass of water $= 18.0\\text{ g mol}^{-1}$)",
     "$11.3\\text{ kJ}$", "$40.7\\text{ kJ}$", "$203.3\\text{ kJ}$", "$2.26\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Moles of water $= 5.0 / 18.0 = 0.2778\\text{ mol}$. Heat required $q = n \\times \\Delta_{\\text{vap}} H = 0.2778 \\times 40.66\\text{ kJ} = 11.29\\text{ kJ} \\approx 11.3\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Vaporization Heat Numerical"),
    ("For the reaction $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4(g) + \\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6(g)$, given $\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4(g)] = +52.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6(g)] = -84.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$, the enthalpy of hydrogenation is:",
     "$-137.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+137.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-32.4\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+32.4\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "$\\Delta_r H^\\circ = \\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6] - \\left(\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4] + \\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{H}_2]\\right) = -84.7 - (52.3 + 0) = -84.7 - 52.3 = -137.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Enthalpy of Hydrogenation of Ethylene"),
    ("Given:\n$\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 3\\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe}(s) + 3\\text{CO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -26.8\\text{ kJ}$\n$\\text{FeO}(s) + \\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -16.5\\text{ kJ}$\nWhat is the enthalpy change for $\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{FeO}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)$?",
     "$+6.2\\text{ kJ}$", "$-43.3\\text{ kJ}$", "$-10.3\\text{ kJ}$", "$+16.5\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Target reaction $= \\text{Equation (1)} - 2 \\times \\text{Equation (2)}$. Thus $\\Delta H = -26.8 - 2(-16.5) = -26.8 + 33.0 = +6.2\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Hess's Law Iron Oxides"),
    ("The standard enthalpy of formation of $\\text{NH}_3(g)$ is $-46.1\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. What is the standard enthalpy change for the decomposition reaction: $2\\text{NH}_3(g) \\rightarrow \\text{N}_2(g) + 3\\text{H}_2(g)$?",
     "$+92.2\\text{ kJ}$", "$-92.2\\text{ kJ}$", "$+46.1\\text{ kJ}$", "$-46.1\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Formation reaction: $\\frac{1}{2}\\text{N}_2 + \\frac{3}{2}\\text{H}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{NH}_3$, $\\Delta_f H = -46.1\\text{ kJ}$. The target reaction is the reverse multiplied by $2$: $\\Delta_r H = -2 \\times \\Delta_f H = -2(-46.1) = +92.2\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Decomposition Enthalpy of Ammonia"),
    ("The heat of combustion of carbon monoxide is $-283.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. How much heat is released when $56\\text{ g}$ of $\\text{CO}$ is completely burned in excess oxygen? (Molar mass of $\\text{CO} = 28\\text{ g mol}^{-1}$)",
     "$566.0\\text{ kJ}$", "$283.0\\text{ kJ}$", "$141.5\\text{ kJ}$", "$1132.0\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Moles of $\\text{CO} = 56 / 28 = 2.0\\text{ moles}$. Total heat released $= 2.0\\text{ mol} \\times 283.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1} = 566.0\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "CO Combustion Heat Release"),
    ("For the reaction $\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{HCl}(g)$, $\\Delta_r H^\\circ = -184.6\\text{ kJ}$. What is $\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{HCl}(g)]$?",
     "$-92.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+92.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-184.6\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+184.6\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "Standard enthalpy of formation is defined per mole of substance formed: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ = \\frac{-184.6\\text{ kJ}}{2\\text{ mol}} = -92.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "HCl Formation Enthalpy"),
    ("Kirchhoff's equation in integral form over temperature range $T_1$ to $T_2$ is:",
     "$\\Delta H_{T_2} = \\Delta H_{T_1} + \\int_{T_1}^{T_2} \\Delta C_p dT$", "$\\Delta H_{T_2} = \\Delta H_{T_1} - \\int_{T_1}^{T_2} \\Delta C_p dT$", "$\\Delta H_{T_2} = \\Delta H_{T_1} + \\Delta C_v (T_2 - T_1)$", "$\\Delta H_{T_2} = \\Delta H_{T_1} \\times \\frac{T_2}{T_1}$", "A",
     "Differentiating enthalpy with respect to temperature at constant pressure yields $\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\Delta H}{\\partial T}\\right)_P = \\Delta C_p$. Integrating from $T_1$ to $T_2$ gives $\\Delta H_{T_2} = \\Delta H_{T_1} + \\int_{T_1}^{T_2} \\Delta C_p dT$.",
     "Kirchhoff's Integral Equation"),
    ("For the reaction $\\text{N}_2(g) + 3\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{NH}_3(g)$, if $C_p[\\text{N}_2] = 29.1$, $C_p[\\text{H}_2] = 28.8$, and $C_p[\\text{NH}_3] = 35.1\\text{ J K}^{-1}\\text{ mol}^{-1}$, what is $\\Delta C_p$ for the reaction?",
     "$-45.3\\text{ J K}^{-1}$", "$+45.3\\text{ J K}^{-1}$", "$-22.8\\text{ J K}^{-1}$", "$+22.8\\text{ J K}^{-1}$", "A",
     "$\\Delta C_p = 2 C_p[\\text{NH}_3] - (C_p[\\text{N}_2] + 3 C_p[\\text{H}_2]) = 2(35.1) - [29.1 + 3(28.8)] = 70.2 - [29.1 + 86.4] = 70.2 - 115.5 = -45.3\\text{ J K}^{-1}$.",
     "Delta Cp Calculation for Haber Process"),
    ("The standard heats of formation of $\\text{CH}_4(g)$, $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_6(g)$, and $\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_8(g)$ are $-74.8$, $-84.7$, and $-103.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ respectively. What is the trend in stability of these alkanes relative to their constituent elements?",
     "Propane $>$ Ethane $>$ Methane (all exothermic)", "Methane $>$ Ethane $>$ Propane", "All are endothermic", "They have equal stability", "A",
     "All three have negative $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ values, meaning they are exothermic and thermodynamically more stable than their free elements. Propane has the most negative value ($-103.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$), releasing the most heat upon formation per mole.",
     "Alkane Enthalpy of Formation Trend"),
    ("Given: $\\Delta H$ of combustion of graphite is $-393.5\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ and diamond is $-395.4\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. The enthalpy change for the transition $\\text{C}(\\text{graphite}) \\rightarrow \\text{C}(\\text{diamond})$ is:",
     "$+1.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-1.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+788.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-788.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "$\\text{C}(\\text{graphite}) + \\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2$, $\\Delta H = -393.5\\text{ kJ}$. $\\text{C}(\\text{diamond}) + \\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2$, $\\Delta H = -395.4\\text{ kJ}$. Subtracting gives $\\text{C}(\\text{graphite}) \\rightarrow \\text{C}(\\text{diamond})$, $\\Delta H = -393.5 - (-395.4) = +1.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Graphite to Diamond Transition Enthalpy", True, "2013"),
    ("The enthalpy of combustion of benzoic acid at $298\\text{ K}$ is $-3227\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. If $0.61\\text{ g}$ of benzoic acid (molar mass $122\\text{ g mol}^{-1}$) is burned, how much heat is released?",
     "$16.14\\text{ kJ}$", "$32.27\\text{ kJ}$", "$8.07\\text{ kJ}$", "$64.54\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Moles $= 0.61 / 122 = 0.0050\\text{ mol}$. Heat released $= 0.0050\\text{ mol} \\times 3227\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1} = 16.135\\text{ kJ} \\approx 16.14\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Benzoic Acid Combustion Numerical"),
    ("For the reaction $\\text{C}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CH}_4(g)$, $\\Delta_r H = -74.8\\text{ kJ}$. What is $\\Delta_r U$ at $298\\text{ K}$?",
     "$-72.32\\text{ kJ}$", "$-77.28\\text{ kJ}$", "$-74.80\\text{ kJ}$", "$+72.32\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "$\\Delta n_g = 1 - 2 = -1$. $\\Delta_r H = \\Delta_r U + \\Delta n_g RT \\implies \\Delta_r U = \\Delta_r H - \\Delta n_g RT = -74.8 - [(-1)(8.314 \\times 10^{-3})(298)] = -74.8 - (-2.478) = -72.32\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Delta U for Methane Formation"),
    ("Given that $\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s)] = -1675.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s)] = -824.2\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$, the enthalpy change for the thermite reaction $2\\text{Al}(s) + \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) \\rightarrow \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 2\\text{Fe}(s)$ is:",
     "$-851.5\\text{ kJ}$", "$+851.5\\text{ kJ}$", "$-2499.9\\text{ kJ}$", "$+2499.9\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "$\\Delta_r H^\\circ = \\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3] - \\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3] = -1675.7 - (-824.2) = -1675.7 + 824.2 = -851.5\\text{ kJ}$. This highly exothermic reaction is used for welding railway tracks.",
     "Thermite Reaction Enthalpy"),
    ("Standard enthalpy of formation of $\\text{CS}_2(l)$ can be calculated from:\n(1) $\\text{CS}_2(l) + 3\\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2(g) + 2\\text{SO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -1108\\text{ kJ}$\n(2) $\\text{C}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -393.5\\text{ kJ}$\n(3) $\\text{S}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{SO}_2(g)$, $\\Delta H = -296.8\\text{ kJ}$\nWhat is $\\Delta_f H^\\circ[\\text{CS}_2(l)]$?",
     "$+120.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-120.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+87.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-87.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "Target: $\\text{C} + 2\\text{S} \\rightarrow \\text{CS}_2$. Using Hess's law: $\\Delta_f H^\\circ = \\Delta H_2 + 2\\Delta H_3 - \\Delta H_1 = -393.5 + 2(-296.8) - (-1108) = -393.5 - 593.6 + 1108 = -987.1 + 1108 = +120.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "CS2 Formation Enthalpy Calculation"),
    ("The enthalpy of combustion of glucose $\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6(s)$ is $-2808\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. How much energy is released by the combustion of $36.0\\text{ g}$ of glucose? (Molar mass of glucose $= 180\\text{ g mol}^{-1}$)",
     "$561.6\\text{ kJ}$", "$280.8\\text{ kJ}$", "$1123.2\\text{ kJ}$", "$56.16\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Moles of glucose $= 36.0 / 180 = 0.20\\text{ mol}$. Heat released $= 0.20\\text{ mol} \\times 2808\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1} = 561.6\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Glucose Combustion Energy Numerical"),
    ("The heat of atomization of $\\text{CH}_4(g)$ is $1665\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. What is the average $\\text{C}-\\text{H}$ bond enthalpy in methane?",
     "$416.25\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$832.50\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$1665.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$208.12\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "Methane has 4 identical $\\text{C}-\\text{H}$ bonds: $\\text{CH}_4(g) \\rightarrow \\text{C}(g) + 4\\text{H}(g)$, with $\\Delta_a H = 1665\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. Average $\\text{C}-\\text{H}$ bond enthalpy $= \\frac{1665}{4} = 416.25\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Average CH Bond Enthalpy"),
    ("Given: $\\text{N}_2(g) + 3\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{NH}_3(g)$, $\\Delta_r H_{298}^\\circ = -92.2\\text{ kJ}$. If $\\Delta C_p = -45.0\\text{ J K}^{-1}$, what is $\\Delta_r H^\\circ$ at $398\\text{ K}$?",
     "$-96.7\\text{ kJ}$", "$-87.7\\text{ kJ}$", "$-92.2\\text{ kJ}$", "$-137.2\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Using Kirchhoff's equation: $\\Delta H_{T_2} = \\Delta H_{T_1} + \\Delta C_p (T_2 - T_1)$. $\\Delta T = 398 - 298 = 100\\text{ K}$. $\\Delta C_p \\Delta T = (-45.0\\text{ J K}^{-1})(100\\text{ K}) = -4500\\text{ J} = -4.5\\text{ kJ}$. Thus $\\Delta H_{398} = -92.2 + (-4.5) = -96.7\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "Kirchhoff's Temperature Variation Calculation"),
    ("Which of the following compounds has the standard enthalpy of combustion EQUAL to its standard enthalpy of formation?",
     "$\\text{CO}_2(g)$", "$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$", "None (both are products of combustion)", "$\\text{C}(\\text{graphite})$", "A",
     "Carbon dioxide cannot be burned further; its formation from graphite and $\\text{O}_2$ is the combustion of graphite, but $\\text{CO}_2$ itself does not undergo combustion.",
     "Combustion Concept Analysis"),
    ("Enthalpy of neutralization of a strong acid with a strong base in dilute aqueous solution at $298\\text{ K}$ is constant and equal to:",
     "$-57.1\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-13.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+57.1\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-100.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "For any strong acid and strong base, neutralization is simply the combination of $\\text{H}^+(aq) + \\text{OH}^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$, for which $\\Delta_{\\text{neut}} H^\\circ = -57.1\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ (or $-13.7\\text{ kcal mol}^{-1}$).",
     "Enthalpy of Neutralization Strong Acid Base"),
    ("When $1\\text{ mole}$ of weak acid $\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$ is neutralized by $1\\text{ mole}$ of strong base $\\text{NaOH}$, the heat released is $-55.2\\text{ kJ}$. The enthalpy of ionization of $\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$ is:",
     "$+1.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-1.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+55.2\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+57.1\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "Heat of neutralization of strong acid/strong base is $-57.1\\text{ kJ}$. Part of the heat is consumed in ionizing the weak acetic acid: $\\Delta H_{\\text{neut}} = -57.1 + \\Delta H_{\\text{ion}} \\implies -55.2 = -57.1 + \\Delta H_{\\text{ion}} \\implies \\Delta H_{\\text{ion}} = -55.2 + 57.1 = +1.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Enthalpy of Ionization of Weak Acid"),
    ("The enthalpy of solution of $\\text{CaCl}_2(s)$ in water is negative (exothermic). When anhydrous $\\text{CaCl}_2$ dissolves in water at room temperature, the temperature of the solution will:",
     "Increase", "Decrease", "Remain unchanged", "Drop to $0^\\circ\\text{C}$", "A",
     "Because $\\Delta_{\\text{sol}} H < 0$ (heat is liberated into the aqueous medium), the dissolution of anhydrous $\\text{CaCl}_2$ raises the temperature of the water.",
     "Exothermic Dissolution"),
    ("The enthalpy of solution of $\\text{NH}_4\\text{NO}_3(s)$ in water is positive (endothermic). When $\\text{NH}_4\\text{NO}_3$ dissolves in water, the temperature of the solution:",
     "Decreases", "Increases", "Remains constant", "Reaches boiling point", "A",
     "An endothermic dissolution absorbs heat from the surrounding water, causing the temperature of the solution to fall (the principle used in instant cold packs).",
     "Endothermic Dissolution"),
    ("Given that the heat of sublimation of iodine is $62.4\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$ and the heat of fusion is $15.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$, what is the heat of vaporization of liquid iodine?",
     "$+46.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+78.1\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-46.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+31.2\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "$\\Delta_{\\text{sub}} H = \\Delta_{\\text{fus}} H + \\Delta_{\\text{vap}} H \\implies \\Delta_{\\text{vap}} H = \\Delta_{\\text{sub}} H - \\Delta_{\\text{fus}} H = 62.4 - 15.7 = +46.7\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Heat of Vaporization from Sublimation"),
    ("How many grams of methane must be burned to produce $4451.5\\text{ kJ}$ of heat? (Given: $\\Delta_c H^\\circ[\\text{CH}_4] = -890.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$, molar mass of $\\text{CH}_4 = 16.0\\text{ g mol}^{-1}$)",
     "$80.0\\text{ g}$", "$16.0\\text{ g}$", "$40.0\\text{ g}$", "$160.0\\text{ g}$", "A",
     "Moles of $\\text{CH}_4$ required $= \\frac{4451.5\\text{ kJ}}{890.3\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}} = 5.0\\text{ moles}$. Mass of $\\text{CH}_4 = 5.0 \\times 16.0 = 80.0\\text{ g}$.",
     "Fuel Mass Calculation from Combustion"),
    ("For the reaction $\\text{CO}_2(g) + \\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CO}(g) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)$, $\\Delta H = +41.2\\text{ kJ}$. This reaction is:",
     "Endothermic, favored by higher temperatures according to Le Chatelier's principle", "Exothermic, favored by lower temperatures", "Athermal ($\\Delta H = 0$)", "Spontaneous at all temperatures", "A",
     "Because $\\Delta H > 0$, the forward reaction is endothermic. According to Le Chatelier's principle, increasing the temperature shifts endothermic equilibria to the right.",
     "Endothermic Reaction Temperature Effect"),
    ("The standard enthalpy of formation of liquid benzene is $+49.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. The positive value indicates that benzene is:",
     "Thermodynamically endothermic relative to graphite and $\\text{H}_2(g)$", "More stable than $\\text{CO}_2$ and $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$", "Incapable of combustion", "A gas at standard temperature", "A",
     "A positive $\\Delta_f H^\\circ$ means that forming benzene from standard state elements requires energy input, making benzene an endothermic compound.",
     "Benzene Formation Enthalpy Sign"),
    ("Given:\n(1) $\\text{H}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$, $\\Delta H = -285.8\\text{ kJ}$\n(2) $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)$, $\\Delta H = +44.0\\text{ kJ}$\nWhat is the enthalpy of formation of gaseous water $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)$?",
     "$-241.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$-329.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+241.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "$+44.0\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$", "A",
     "Adding the two equations: $\\text{H}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(g)$. By Hess's law, $\\Delta_f H^\\circ = -285.8 + (+44.0) = -241.8\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$.",
     "Formation of Steam via Hess's Law"),
    ("The enthalpy of combustion of butane ($\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_{10}$) is $-2878\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}$. A domestic LPG cylinder contains $14.2\\text{ kg}$ of butane. What is the total energy stored in the cylinder? (Molar mass of butane $= 58.1\\text{ g mol}^{-1}$)",
     "$7.03 \\times 10^5\\text{ kJ}$", "$2.88 \\times 10^5\\text{ kJ}$", "$1.42 \\times 10^6\\text{ kJ}$", "$4.09 \\times 10^4\\text{ kJ}$", "A",
     "Moles of butane $= \\frac{14200\\text{ g}}{58.1\\text{ g mol}^{-1}} = 244.4\\text{ mol}$. Total energy $= 244.4 \\times 2878\\text{ kJ} = 703383\\text{ kJ} \\approx 7.03 \\times 10^5\\text{ kJ}$.",
     "LPG Cylinder Energy Content Calculation"),
